  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find all positive integers \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) divides the product \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Divisibility Condition\nWe need \\( n + 2 \\) to divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). This means that:\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{n + 2}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the Expression\nFirst, let's simplify \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9)\n\\]\nNotice that \\( n^2 + 9 \\) can be written as \\( (n + 3)(n - 3) + 18 \\), but this might not be immediately helpful. Instead, let's consider the divisibility condition directly.\n\n### Step 3: Apply the Divisibility Condition\nSince \\( n + 2 \\) divides \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\), we can write:\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = k(n + 2)\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\n### Step 4: Test Small Values of \\( n \\)\nLet's test small positive integers for \\( n \\) to see which ones satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 1: \\( n = 1 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 3\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(4)(10) = 120\n\\]\n\\[\n120 \\div 3 = 40\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 1 \\) satisfies the condition.\n\n#### Case 2: \\( n = 2 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(5)(13) = 195\n\\]\n\\[\n195 \\div 4 = 48.75\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 2 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 3: \\( n = 3 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(6)(18) = 324\n\\]\n\\[\n324 \\div 5 = 64.8\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 3 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 4: \\( n = 4 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(7)(25) = 525\n\\]\n\\[\n525 \\div 6 = 87.5\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 4 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 5: \\( n = 5 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(8)(26) = 624\n\\]\n\\[\n624 \\div 7 = 89.1428...\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 5 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 6: \\( n = 6 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(9)(33) = 891\n\\]\n\\[\n891 \\div 8 = 111.375\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 6 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 7: \\( n = 7 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(10)(40) = 1200\n\\]\n\\[\n1200 \\div 9 = 133.333...\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 7 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 8: \\( n = 8 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(11)(49) = 1617\n\\]\n\\[\n1617 \\div 10 = 161.7\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 8 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 9: \\( n = 9 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 11\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(12)(60) = 2160\n\\]\n\\[\n2160 \\div 11 = 196.3636...\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 9 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n#### Case 10: \\( n = 10 \\)\n\\[\nn + 2 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(13)(75) = 2925\n\\]\n\\[\n2925 \\div 12 = 243.75\n\\]\nSo, \\( n = 10 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n### Step 5: Conclusion\nFrom the above cases, the only positive integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is \\( n = 1 \\).\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
